#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186156993 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157392 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38455e74-50c2-5581-b9e1-a8dd0c85f826" :knowledge-point/title "The Expected Value of the Sample Mean"}] :question/id #uuid "33bedb30-2c99-52e8-a665-e3bddeed7e13" :question/math-academy-id "e-10114" :question/problem "A sample $X_1, X_2$ of two metallic rings is drawn from a population. The population has rings of two different diameters, $16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}$ and $\\textrm{18}\\,\\textrm{mm}$, distributed in the ratio $5:1$. Calculate $\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}]$." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with population mean $\\mu$. Then, the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} = \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i\n$$\n\nSince $\\overline{X}$ is an unbiased estimator of $\\mu$, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu\n$$\n\nWe're told that the rings in the population have diameters $16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}$ and $\\textrm{18}\\,\\textrm{mm}$, and these are distributed in the ratio $5:1$. Therefore, to compute the population mean, we can consider a population consisting of\n\n$$\n5+1 = 6\n$$\n\nrings only with the following diameters:\n\n$$\n16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}, \\qquad 16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}, \\qquad 16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}, \\qquad 16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}, \\qquad 16.5\\,\\textrm{mm}, \\qquad 18\\,\\textrm{mm}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nμ &= \\frac{1}{6}∑_(i)x_{i} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}(5 \\cdot 16.5 + 18) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{6}(82.5 + 18) \\\\\n&= \\frac{100.5}{6} \\\\\n&= 16.75mm\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[\\overline{X}] = \\mu = 16.75\\,\\textrm{mm}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157172 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157355 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b60ce582-4127-57b8-bba8-04cf17169ca3" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Population Mean From the Sample Mean"}] :question/id #uuid "a485378b-0bce-5576-bcbc-2ef77df2da17" :question/math-academy-id "e-7882" :question/problem "Suppose the following data is sampled from a population. Compute an estimate for the population mean.\n\n$6, \\quad 13, \\quad 7, \\quad 5, \\quad 14, \\quad 11, \\quad 7$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with the population mean $\\mu$.\nThen, the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overline{X} = \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n X_i\n$$\n\nNote that:\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ is the mean value of $X_1,X_2,\\ldots, X_n$, while $\\bar{x}$ is the mean of a specific sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$,\n- the sample mean $\\overline{X}$ is an estimator of the population mean $\\mu$, and\n- the value $\\hat{\\mu} = \\bar{x}$ is an estimate of the population mean $\\mu$.\n\nTherefore, our estimate for the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nμ^(^) &= x^(―) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{n}∑_(i = 1)^(7)x_{i} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{7}(6 + 13 + 7 + 5 + 14 + 11 + 7) \\\\\n&= \\frac{63}{7} \\\\\n&= 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157599 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157392 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38455e74-50c2-5581-b9e1-a8dd0c85f826" :knowledge-point/title "The Expected Value of the Sample Mean"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be53242b-fb33-52fe-8abb-05850015e594" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8cm" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153839} {:answer/id #uuid "d4f82bf8-d34a-56b2-a757-96d3d158f1c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.5cm" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153840} {:answer/id #uuid "e516c404-4e3f-50f0-a2f0-00c30473ff72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.5cm" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153841} {:answer/id #uuid "13decd35-9de1-5415-abd1-3bf92030ab86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.25cm" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153842} {:answer/id #uuid "2bacfedd-983a-5218-84f8-f3ba2c209f05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7cm" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153843}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a5331396-94ef-5efd-8765-b14ebeb28e6f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186153838}] :question/id #uuid "1ea5fc2c-4d2b-5104-893c-7896d0999b42" :question/math-academy-id "q-143190" :question/problem "A sample $X_{1}, X_{2}$ of two nails is drawn from a population. The population has nails of two different lengths, $6cm$ and $12cm$, distributed in the ratio $3: 1$. Calculate $E[X^(―)]$.\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true}]}
